WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:02.200
Hello, and thanks for watching. In

00:02.200 --> 00:03.919
this presentation, we introduce our

00:03.919 --> 00:05.960
work metonymic circuits for

00:05.960 --> 00:07.759
abstract concept grounding in

00:07.759 --> 00:10.160
vision transformers. We investigate

00:10.160 --> 00:12.360
how vision transformers internally

00:12.360 --> 00:14.440
ground abstract concepts and

00:14.440 --> 00:16.279
whether they rely on more concrete

00:16.279 --> 00:18.359
visual concepts as intermediate

00:18.359 --> 00:19.120
anchors.

00:19.902 --> 00:22.061
Let me start with a question.

00:22.061 --> 00:23.982
Take a look at this icon.

00:23.982 --> 00:25.902
What do you think it means?

00:25.902 --> 00:27.301
You can probably all tell

00:27.301 --> 00:29.222
immediately. This represents

00:29.222 --> 00:32.022
something like angry. But here's

00:32.022 --> 00:33.782
the thing. There's nothing in the

00:33.782 --> 00:37.222
pixels that literally is angry.

00:37.222 --> 00:40.181
Angry is an abstract concept. You

00:40.181 --> 00:42.021
can't point to a pixel and say

00:42.021 --> 00:43.662
that's angry.

00:43.662 --> 00:45.582
What you actually see are these

00:45.582 --> 00:48.261
concrete visual cues, a pinkish

00:48.261 --> 00:51.622
color, an upset face and fire.

00:51.622 --> 00:52.782
These are all things you can

00:52.782 --> 00:55.621
directly point to in the image.

00:55.621 --> 00:57.822
And somehow you combine them to

00:57.822 --> 01:01.182
infer the abstract meaning angry.

01:01.182 --> 01:02.941
This corresponds to a concept in

01:02.941 --> 01:04.621
cognitive linguistics called

01:04.621 --> 01:06.702
metronomy. The idea that we

01:06.702 --> 01:08.902
understand abstract concepts through

01:08.902 --> 01:10.742
concrete proxies within the same

01:10.742 --> 01:12.461
conceptual domain.

01:12.461 --> 01:14.742
Fire doesn't mean anger, but it

01:14.742 --> 01:16.542
evokes anger through conventional

01:16.542 --> 01:18.022
association.

01:18.022 --> 01:20.421
So, our core hypothesis is

01:20.421 --> 01:22.902
The grounding of abstract concepts

01:22.902 --> 01:24.262
depends on the grounding of

01:24.262 --> 01:26.742
intermediate more concrete concepts.

01:26.743 --> 01:28.823
Now you have a hypothesis of how

01:28.823 --> 01:30.702
you know what it means.

01:30.702 --> 01:32.463
A natural question is, does a

01:32.463 --> 01:34.423
vision transformer do something

01:34.423 --> 01:37.223
similar? Inside the model, can we

01:37.223 --> 01:40.342
find some concepts like pink, face

01:40.342 --> 01:41.703
and emotion?

01:41.744 --> 01:43.744
The answer is yes. We can do

01:43.744 --> 01:46.224
this by building a circuit inside

01:46.224 --> 01:48.063
the model with each node

01:48.063 --> 01:50.343
representing some concepts. The

01:50.343 --> 01:52.984
tool we use here is Transcoder. A

01:52.984 --> 01:54.343
node in the circuit is a

01:54.343 --> 01:55.904
Transcoder feature.

01:57.617 --> 01:59.496
Let's see how it works. This is

01:59.496 --> 02:01.857
a normal MLP layer in CLIP. We

02:01.857 --> 02:03.577
add a Transcoder alongside the

02:03.577 --> 02:06.896
MLP. It takes the same MLP input,

02:06.896 --> 02:08.377
but it projects it into a much

02:08.377 --> 02:11.136
higher dimensional space, 64 times

02:11.136 --> 02:13.297
the MLP's dimensions.

02:13.297 --> 02:15.097
If you are familiar with SAE, the

02:15.097 --> 02:16.776
idea is the same.

02:16.776 --> 02:18.577
Spread the information across many

02:18.577 --> 02:20.297
more dimensions so that each

02:20.297 --> 02:21.657
dimension is sparse and

02:21.657 --> 02:23.297
interpretable.

02:23.297 --> 02:25.097
The architecture is straightforward.

02:25.097 --> 02:27.337
It has an encoder and a decoder.

02:27.337 --> 02:29.896
The MLP input X goes through the

02:29.896 --> 02:32.297
encoder matrix, then a relu

02:32.297 --> 02:33.857
activation.

02:33.857 --> 02:35.617
Here, we get a sparse activation

02:35.617 --> 02:39.097
vector A of SAE dimension.

02:39.097 --> 02:40.697
Each element of this vector is an

02:40.697 --> 02:42.697
activation value of a Transcoder

02:42.697 --> 02:44.777
feature. Intuitively, this

02:44.777 --> 02:46.297
represents how much a feature

02:46.297 --> 02:48.617
should be activated given this MLP

02:48.617 --> 02:49.817
input.

02:49.817 --> 02:51.416
Then the decoder matrix projects

02:51.416 --> 02:53.017
it back to the original dimension

02:53.017 --> 02:54.977
to produce the output.

02:54.977 --> 02:56.896
Intuitively, this represents what a

02:56.896 --> 02:58.177
feature should output to the

02:58.177 --> 02:59.736
residual stream.

02:59.736 --> 03:01.697
The training loss has two terms.

03:01.697 --> 03:03.177
The first is a reconstruction

03:03.177 --> 03:04.816
loss. We want the Transcoder

03:04.816 --> 03:06.377
output to be close to the actual

03:06.377 --> 03:08.537
MLP output. The second is a

03:08.537 --> 03:09.937
sparsity penalty on the

03:09.937 --> 03:12.177
activations. We want most features

03:12.177 --> 03:13.657
to be inactive for any given

03:13.657 --> 03:14.417
input.

03:14.418 --> 03:16.498
So, based on this architecture, we

03:16.498 --> 03:19.378
have two takeaways. Number one,

03:19.378 --> 03:21.177
due to the sparsity penalty and

03:21.177 --> 03:23.738
high dimensionality, each Transcoder

03:23.738 --> 03:25.578
feature is approximately

03:25.578 --> 03:27.097
mono-semicantic.

03:27.097 --> 03:28.858
This is a standard assumption in

03:28.858 --> 03:30.858
sparse dictionary learning.

03:30.858 --> 03:33.617
Number two, the encoder reads from

03:33.617 --> 03:35.818
the residual stream. It determines

03:35.818 --> 03:37.498
how strongly each feature should

03:37.498 --> 03:40.058
be activated by the current input.

03:40.058 --> 03:41.537
The decoder writes back to the

03:41.537 --> 03:43.578
residual stream. Once a feature is

03:43.578 --> 03:45.777
activated, Its decoder vector

03:45.777 --> 03:47.538
determines what information it

03:47.538 --> 03:49.137
contributes to downstream

03:49.137 --> 03:50.178
computation.

03:50.936 --> 03:53.016
After these two steps, here's the

03:53.016 --> 03:54.696
key insight. Under these

03:54.696 --> 03:56.656
approximations, the model becomes

03:56.656 --> 03:59.096
approximately linear and that means

03:59.096 --> 04:01.096
we can directly compute how any

04:01.096 --> 04:03.336
Transcoder feature influences any

04:03.336 --> 04:06.376
other feature or the final logit.

04:06.376 --> 04:08.096
Specifically, how a feature

04:08.096 --> 04:09.816
contributes to the final logit can

04:09.816 --> 04:11.256
be computed as the partial

04:11.256 --> 04:13.055
derivative of the logit with

04:13.055 --> 04:14.856
respect to the features activation

04:14.856 --> 04:15.936
value.

04:15.936 --> 04:17.856
Similarly, we can compute how a

04:17.856 --> 04:19.576
former feature contributes to a

04:19.576 --> 04:20.975
later feature.

04:20.975 --> 04:22.455
And importantly, this can be

04:22.455 --> 04:25.256
across non-adjacent layers.

04:25.256 --> 04:27.015
These attribution values become the

04:27.015 --> 04:29.136
edge weights in our circuit.

04:29.136 --> 04:30.376
So the final product is a

04:30.376 --> 04:32.616
directed acyclic graph from the

04:32.616 --> 04:34.136
embedding through Transcoder

04:34.136 --> 04:35.975
features across layers to the

04:35.975 --> 04:38.335
logit. Each edge is weighted by

04:38.335 --> 04:39.656
the attribution score.

04:39.897 --> 04:41.537
We now have a circuit with edge

04:41.537 --> 04:43.056
weights, but we need a way to

04:43.056 --> 04:44.976
measure how important each node is

04:44.976 --> 04:47.137
in the circuit. So, here we have

04:47.137 --> 04:49.296
a metric called mass flow. The

04:49.296 --> 04:51.697
idea is very intuitive. We started

04:51.697 --> 04:53.376
the log it and assign it a mass

04:53.376 --> 04:55.496
of one. Then we propagate this

04:55.496 --> 04:57.297
mass backward through the circuit,

04:57.297 --> 04:59.217
distributing it proportionally

04:59.217 --> 05:01.016
according to edge weights. For

05:01.016 --> 05:02.776
each node S, its mass flow is

05:02.776 --> 05:04.696
the sum over all its outgoing

05:04.696 --> 05:07.016
edges of the edge weight fraction

05:07.016 --> 05:09.696
times the target nodes mass flow.

05:09.696 --> 05:10.856
Higher mass flow means that the

05:10.856 --> 05:12.896
feature carries more credit toward

05:12.896 --> 05:13.777
the loget.

05:14.299 --> 05:16.899
Next, we assign human interpretable

05:16.899 --> 05:19.739
labels to the Transcoder features.

05:19.739 --> 05:22.259
For each abstract concept such as

05:22.259 --> 05:24.539
angry, we collect about 200

05:24.539 --> 05:27.139
reference images. GPT annotates

05:27.139 --> 05:29.419
which concrete concepts are present

05:29.419 --> 05:31.899
in each image, while we record

05:31.899 --> 05:33.859
the activation of each Transcoder

05:33.859 --> 05:35.019
feature.

05:35.019 --> 05:36.619
We then compute the sperming

05:36.619 --> 05:38.818
correlation between concept presence

05:38.818 --> 05:40.779
and feature activation across these

05:40.779 --> 05:42.059
images.

05:42.059 --> 05:43.898
The highest correlated words become

05:43.898 --> 05:45.459
the sem- anti-teak labels for that

05:45.459 --> 05:46.698
feature.

05:46.698 --> 05:48.538
We also group these labels into

05:48.538 --> 05:51.059
four grounding types, perceptual,

05:51.059 --> 05:53.899
object, abstract and textual, which

05:53.899 --> 05:55.739
we use later in our analysis.

05:55.740 --> 05:59.820
For the data, we selected 1,355

05:59.820 --> 06:02.260
abstract concepts from the MRC

06:02.260 --> 06:04.740
psychotechnical database. For each

06:04.740 --> 06:07.060
concept, we collected approximately

06:07.060 --> 06:09.980
200 icons from Flatcon and we

06:09.980 --> 06:12.340
split them into three subsets.

06:12.340 --> 06:14.820
Visual icon contains no text of

06:14.820 --> 06:16.179
the target label.

06:16.179 --> 06:18.380
Visual text is mainly rendered

06:18.380 --> 06:20.900
text spelling the label. Mix

06:20.900 --> 06:22.820
contains both text and visual

06:22.820 --> 06:24.060
elements.

06:24.060 --> 06:26.580
We have GPT annotate each image

06:26.580 --> 06:28.420
and give element words of four

06:28.420 --> 06:29.860
grounding types present in the

06:29.860 --> 06:30.940
image.

06:30.940 --> 06:32.940
The data is used in two ways.

06:32.940 --> 06:34.619
The reference set for the total

06:34.619 --> 06:37.340
of 200 images per concept is used

06:37.340 --> 06:39.619
in the GPT annotation and feature

06:39.619 --> 06:41.180
labeling pipeline.

06:41.180 --> 06:43.380
The probe set of 10 images is

06:43.380 --> 06:45.180
used for actual circuit discovery

06:45.180 --> 06:46.140
and tracing.

06:46.421 --> 06:47.701
We run the circuit tracing

06:47.701 --> 06:51.500
pipeline on OpenCLIP ViT-B/32 and

06:51.500 --> 06:54.101
DINOv2-base. For each of the

06:54.101 --> 06:56.941
three subsets, visual icon, mix

06:56.941 --> 06:59.660
and visual text, we use 1000

06:59.660 --> 07:02.381
images covering 100 concepts with

07:02.381 --> 07:04.141
10 images per concept.

07:04.382 --> 07:06.301
Here are the results. For brevity,

07:06.301 --> 07:08.061
we show only the CLIP results

07:08.061 --> 07:10.142
here. Let's start with node level

07:10.142 --> 07:12.061
results. How mass flow and

07:12.061 --> 07:13.622
grounding types are distributed

07:13.622 --> 07:14.982
across layers.

07:14.983 --> 07:17.063
The left figure shows mass flow

07:17.063 --> 07:19.423
aggregated by layer. Circuit flow

07:19.423 --> 07:21.302
is heavily concentrated in early

07:21.302 --> 07:23.463
to mid layers and drops sharply

07:23.463 --> 07:25.702
after layer six. So the model's

07:25.702 --> 07:27.502
high contribution computational

07:27.502 --> 07:29.463
roots are established in the first

07:29.463 --> 07:31.022
half of the network.

07:31.022 --> 07:32.543
The right plot breaks down each

07:32.543 --> 07:34.022
layer's mass flow by grounding

07:34.022 --> 07:36.823
type. perceptual features dominate.

07:36.823 --> 07:38.903
The share of object, abstract and

07:38.903 --> 07:40.863
textual features gradually grows

07:40.863 --> 07:41.703
with depth.

07:41.704 --> 07:43.464
The previous slide looked at

07:43.464 --> 07:45.744
individual nodes. Now let's zoom

07:45.744 --> 07:47.664
out to entire paths.

07:47.664 --> 07:50.024
A path are a chain of notes from

07:50.024 --> 07:51.984
embedding to logic. The question

07:51.984 --> 07:54.263
is, how does grounding type evolve

07:54.263 --> 07:55.664
a long a path?

07:55.664 --> 07:57.463
Is it always a clean progression

07:57.463 --> 07:59.784
from perceptual to object to

07:59.784 --> 08:00.504
abstract?

08:00.505 --> 08:03.144
The answer is no. We clustered all

08:03.144 --> 08:04.625
paths by their grounding type

08:04.625 --> 08:06.665
profiles and found several stable

08:06.665 --> 08:08.625
regimes. Here we show two

08:08.625 --> 08:10.625
representative examples.

08:10.625 --> 08:12.465
A first dominant cluster is P

08:12.465 --> 08:14.385
dominant. perceptual features

08:14.385 --> 08:16.184
dominate the entire path. For

08:16.184 --> 08:17.905
friend, every note along the way

08:17.905 --> 08:19.624
is about silhouettes, lines,

08:19.624 --> 08:21.305
shapes. Another one is a

08:21.305 --> 08:24.185
transition PDT. It mostly shows up

08:24.185 --> 08:26.385
in the visual text subset. For

08:26.385 --> 08:27.825
bang, the path goes from

08:27.825 --> 08:29.944
perceptual features like black and

08:29.944 --> 08:32.024
stroke. to textual features like

08:32.024 --> 08:34.185
font. This cluster reveals that

08:34.185 --> 08:36.305
CLIP uses a qualitatively different

08:36.305 --> 08:37.865
strategy when it encounters

08:37.865 --> 08:40.145
readable text. So, the key point

08:40.145 --> 08:42.185
is metonymic grounding is not a

08:42.185 --> 08:44.345
single fixed pipeline. Different

08:44.345 --> 08:46.465
concepts and different image types

08:46.465 --> 08:47.785
activate different grounding

08:47.785 --> 08:48.745
regimes.

08:49.386 --> 08:51.386
We also measured the semantic

08:51.386 --> 08:53.105
similarity between each node's

08:53.105 --> 08:54.705
concept labels and the target

08:54.705 --> 08:56.745
label using a calibrated variant

08:56.745 --> 08:58.586
of bird score and then cluster

08:58.586 --> 08:59.866
paths by the shape of these

08:59.866 --> 09:01.426
alignment curves.

09:01.426 --> 09:03.105
We found that from embedding node

09:03.105 --> 09:04.905
to loget, the semantic meaning is

09:04.905 --> 09:07.386
not always monotonically increasing.

09:07.386 --> 09:08.866
For example, we can see there are

09:08.866 --> 09:10.626
shapes like inverted U and

09:10.626 --> 09:12.706
descending curve. The bar charts

09:12.706 --> 09:14.066
below show the grounding type

09:14.066 --> 09:16.585
composition at each path position.

09:16.585 --> 09:18.026
The alignment shape correlates with

09:18.026 --> 09:19.866
the grounding type dynamics. For

09:19.866 --> 09:21.706
example, in the descending pattern,

09:21.706 --> 09:23.426
the path moves from abstract heavy

09:23.426 --> 09:25.506
early features to perceptual heavy

09:25.506 --> 09:27.306
later ones, which explains why

09:27.306 --> 09:28.866
semantic alignment drops.

09:29.648 --> 09:31.328
But are these circuits actually

09:31.328 --> 09:33.688
causal, rather than just correlated

09:33.688 --> 09:35.568
with the prediction? We test this

09:35.568 --> 09:37.848
with two interventions. On the

09:37.848 --> 09:39.808
left, we progressively ablate

09:39.808 --> 09:41.367
features along high mass flow

09:41.367 --> 09:42.367
paths.

09:42.367 --> 09:43.648
As we disrupt more of the

09:43.648 --> 09:45.728
circuit, prediction loss increases

09:45.728 --> 09:47.528
consistently, showing that these

09:47.528 --> 09:49.408
paths causally contribute to the

09:49.408 --> 09:51.728
output. On the right, we ask what

09:51.728 --> 09:53.007
is needed to recover the

09:53.007 --> 09:55.007
prediction. Removing the circuit

09:55.007 --> 09:56.847
cannot be compensated for by the

09:56.847 --> 09:59.007
remaining features, even when all

09:59.007 --> 10:00.407
of them are restored board.

10:00.407 --> 10:02.207
In contrast, when the circuit is

10:02.207 --> 10:04.408
preserved, adding back only about

10:04.408 --> 10:06.568
3 to 4% of the most active

10:06.568 --> 10:08.448
outside features is enough to

10:08.448 --> 10:11.008
recover nearly the full loget. So

10:11.008 --> 10:12.727
the circuit acts as a necessary

10:12.727 --> 10:14.608
backbone together with a small

10:14.608 --> 10:16.128
amount of distributed supporting

10:16.128 --> 10:17.208
activity.

10:18.287 --> 10:20.208
Finally, could these grounding

10:20.208 --> 10:22.047
patterns simply come from word

10:22.047 --> 10:23.288
co-occurrence in the training

10:23.288 --> 10:24.608
captions?

10:24.608 --> 10:26.568
To test this, we compare two

10:26.568 --> 10:28.288
quantities for each concrete

10:28.288 --> 10:31.408
concept. NPMI measures how strongly

10:31.408 --> 10:33.328
that concept co-occurs with the

10:33.328 --> 10:34.848
abstract label in the training

10:34.848 --> 10:37.367
text, while mass flow measures how

10:37.367 --> 10:39.248
important it is inside the model

10:39.248 --> 10:40.527
circuit.

10:40.527 --> 10:41.928
We then compute Spearman

10:41.928 --> 10:43.408
correlation between these two

10:43.408 --> 10:44.648
rankings.

10:44.648 --> 10:45.968
If the circuit were mainly

10:45.968 --> 10:48.207
reflecting textual co-occurrence,

10:48.207 --> 10:50.367
Concepts with high npmI should

10:50.367 --> 10:52.568
also have high mass flow.

10:52.568 --> 10:54.528
But the correlation is relatively

10:54.528 --> 10:57.048
weak. The mean spermen R is only

10:57.048 --> 11:00.448
around 0.07 across our settings.

11:00.448 --> 11:02.167
And after FDR correction for

11:02.167 --> 11:04.368
multiple comparisons, almost none

11:04.368 --> 11:05.608
of these correlations remain

11:05.608 --> 11:07.008
significant.

11:07.008 --> 11:08.647
So, the circuit structure cannot

11:08.647 --> 11:10.088
be explained by simple word

11:10.088 --> 11:11.608
co-current statistics in the

11:11.608 --> 11:12.928
training captions.

11:14.329 --> 11:16.248
Here is the conclusion.

11:16.248 --> 11:18.008
We proposed the metonymic grounding

11:18.008 --> 11:20.729
hypothesis that vision transformers

11:20.729 --> 11:22.648
ground abstract concepts through

11:22.648 --> 11:25.009
intermediate more concrete visual

11:25.009 --> 11:26.569
concepts.

11:26.569 --> 11:27.969
We developed a method using

11:27.969 --> 11:30.049
Transcoders and circuit tracing on

11:30.049 --> 11:31.969
vision encoder to recover

11:31.969 --> 11:33.969
interpretable computational circuits

11:33.969 --> 11:36.649
for abstract concept recognition.

11:36.649 --> 11:39.329
Our main findings, first structured

11:39.329 --> 11:41.329
metonymic circuits exist with

11:41.329 --> 11:43.049
perceptual primitives in early

11:43.049 --> 11:44.288
layers.

11:44.288 --> 11:45.768
Second, there are multiple

11:45.768 --> 11:47.449
grounding regimes.

11:47.449 --> 11:49.249
Third, circuit importance is

11:49.249 --> 11:51.129
decoupled from training caption

11:51.129 --> 11:52.808
co-occurrence.

11:52.808 --> 11:54.449
Finally, causal interventions

11:54.449 --> 11:55.729
confirmed these circuits are

11:55.729 --> 11:57.808
functionally necessary. Thanks for

11:57.808 --> 11:58.449
watching.
